<h1 class="article-title">Liepāja SEZ Company NorSAF and US-based KBR Sign Licensing Agreement for SAF and eSAF Production in Latvia for the European Market</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>NorSAF, an oil and chemical product storage and transshipment terminal planning to build Northern Europe’s largest sustainable aviation fuel (SAF and eSAF) plant in the Liepāja Special Economic Zone, has signed a perpetual licence agreement with US engineering company KBR for the PureSAF® technology. Developed by Swedish Biofuels AB, with KBR as its exclusive global marketing partner, the technology gives NorSAF exclusive rights to use it and lays the groundwork for Europe’s first commercial-scale plant capable of producing 100% drop-in sustainable aviation fuel without blending it with fossil fuel.</p><p>Unlike conventional sustainable aviation fuels that must be blended with fossil fuel, PureSAF contains aromatic components that make it compatible with modern aircraft engines. This allows the fuel to be used in existing aviation infrastructure without major technical changes.</p><p>The plant is expected to begin operating in 2031 and produce 100,000 tonnes of SAF/eSAF annually. It plans to produce SAF from CO₂ and second-generation bioethanol, and synthetic aviation fuel (eSAF) from green hydrogen and biogenic CO₂. Renewable electricity will power hydrogen production through electrolysis; combining this hydrogen with captured carbon will create a complete green-energy cycle. The process is estimated to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by approximately 83% compared with conventional aviation-fuel production. All feedstocks are planned to be sourced in Europe, strengthening energy independence and industrial resilience.</p><p>The project follows the EU ReFuelEU Aviation framework, which requires a gradual increase in SAF use in aviation: 6% by 2030, including at least 0.7% eSAF, rising to 70% by 2050, including at least 35% eSAF. The fuel produced in Liepāja will supply European airlines, helping reduce their carbon emissions. By developing Liepāja into a sustainable-fuel hub, NorSAF and KBR aim to strengthen Latvia’s role in international aviation.</p><p>“We are pleased to cooperate with KBR to introduce PureSAF technology in Europe. Recent global events have made it clear that energy independence is no longer only an economic issue; it is also a national and regional security priority. By producing sustainable 100% drop-in fuel from European feedstocks, we are creating a resilient and self-sufficient energy ecosystem that reduces dependence on external fossil-fuel markets and strengthens European industry,” says Jānis Kisiels, a member of the NorSAF board.</p><p>“We are proud to be part of this significant project, which will support Latvia’s transition to cleaner aviation and reinforce Europe’s leadership in sustainable-fuel innovation. PureSAF technology makes it possible not only to significantly increase SAF production, but also to use CO₂ and synthesis gas in a single plant to create fully functional aviation fuel without blending it with conventional fuel,” says Jay Ibrahim, President of KBR Sustainable Technology Solutions.</p><p>Further development of the project, including the start of construction in the Port of Liepāja, will depend on securing the necessary support and strategic partners.</p><p>PureSAF was developed by Swedish Biofuels AB, and KBR is the exclusive licensing and commercialisation partner. The technology is currently certified for a 50/50 blend with fossil fuel, while certification for 100% drop-in use is ongoing in Europe. Industry participants expect the necessary approvals from international standards organisation ASTM, which develops technical standards for aviation-fuel producers, could be obtained this year. Test flights by the Swedish military and US Air Force have demonstrated that PureSAF can fully replace conventional jet fuel, behaving like Jet A-1 even without blending. Other SAF technologies generally require mixing with fossil fuel at approximately 10–50%, while PureSAF can be used undiluted, potentially reducing petroleum dependence and helping decarbonise aviation.</p><p>About NorSAF</p><p>NorSAF is a Latvia-based liquid petroleum and chemical storage and transshipment terminal and sustainable aviation fuel developer. Together with Avia Solutions Group, one of the world’s largest full-service aircraft leasing providers, it is developing the first world-class SAF and synthetic SAF (eSAF) production facility in the Baltics, within the Liepāja SEZ. The project is valued at more than EUR 1 billion. NorSAF will use Avia Solutions Group’s aviation infrastructure, including Baltic Ground Services’ expertise in SAF supply and distribution.</p><p>About KBR</p><p>KBR supplies science, technology and engineering solutions to governments and businesses worldwide, including NASA and the US armed forces. Approximately 36,000 employees serve customers in more than 80 countries, with operations in 28 countries. KBR specialises in technology, high-value services and long-term operations and maintenance solutions.</p></div>
